 "derivation": "Both forms are real and correctly placed. The PIE reduplicated present *dé-deh₃-ti gave Proto-Indo-Iranian *dádaHti, whence Sanskrit dádāti 'gives', Avestan dadāiti and Old Persian imv. dadātuv 'let him give' (Darius, DNa 55: 'aitamaiy Auramazdā dadātuv'); Nourai's citation form 'dā' is the root as Sanskrit and Avestan grammarians cite it. The agent noun *deh₃-tér- 'giver' gave Skt. dātár-, Av. dātar-, Gk. δωτήρ, Lat. dator. The second gloss 'God' is right only through a second layer: Bartholomae's Avestan article dātar- : dāθr- has (1) 'der, die gibt, schenkt' = Skt. dātar-, and (2) 'der schaffend hervorbringt, Schöpfer', used of Ahura Mazdā and equated by him with Skt. dhātār- — i.e. the divine sense comes from the homophonous root *dʰeh₁-, which Iranian could no longer keep apart from *deh₃-.",
