n.append({
 "id": 5, "lang": "Persian", "words": "day «دی ۲»; Esfandîyâr «اسفندیار»",
 "verdict": "confirmed",
 "derivation": "دی Day is the direct continuation of the Avestan genitive daθušō '(month/day) of the Creator': Av. dāθuš- > MP Day, which MacKenzie glosses 'Day [ddw' | M dyy, N ~] Creator; cal. 10th month; 8th, 15th, 23rd days' — precisely Nourai's 'creator, the tenth month'. The Zoroastrian calendar named the tenth month and the 8th, 15th and 23rd days after Ahura Mazdā under this epithet, which is why Persian still distinguishes دی‌بآذر، دی‌بمهر، دی‌بدین. اسفندیار continues MP Spandadāt < Av. spəntō.dāta- 'given by the Holy One', with the regular Iranian shift of intervocalic -d- of the second member to -y-/-r- in the New Persian name (spandadāt > *spandayār > esfandiyār); Mo'in's footnote in Borhan-e Qate' p. 2396 states this explicitly: 'اما درخصوص اسفندیار باید گفت که آن از شکل قدیم‌تر -dāta آمده و در فارسی «ر» بجای «د» نشسته است'. The chart's NOTE ('see root Ghei 1 for another possible derivation of day') is the alternative that takes دی 'yesterday, past' from *dyew-/*ǰi- — that is a different, homonymous word (Mo'in lists it separately as «دِی [په.] روز گذشته»), so both entries can stand side by side.",
